I'll read the review brief first, then the blinded bundle, and answer only from the protocol-engineer lens.{"lens":"Protocol engineer / implementer","verdict":"changes_requested","summary":"The table is not operable as an integration or code spec: no formal grammar, conflicting enums, undecidable element boundaries, and section-10 formulas are mostly element bags without checkable typed bonds.","findings":[{"id":"F1","severity":"high","section":"§4, §7, §10","claim":"53 core elements + isotope syntax + four bond types suffice to write protocol formulas; worked examples demonstrate the notation.","problem":"Formulas for three protocols absent from §10 run out of notation. Ethena USDe: forced free isotope Cd{backing=delta-neutral}; no element for delta-neutral/CEX-margin backing or off-exchange settlement—large residue. Pendle v2: L5-ish Py+(Sh|Ix|Rb)+Ep+Rd fits the split, but SY adapters and the time-decaying yield-space PT AMM are not Cp/Wg/St/Cl/Pm (Tw is provisional only)—must invent or residue. EigenLayer: Rs+Bs+Au misses multi-AVS slash composition, operator-set economics (Au is call-scope, not stake-to-operator), and redistribution-vs-burn; no operator for Rs securing {service1..n}.","fix":"Publish a closed formula grammar; an allowed isotope-key registry per element; composition operators for multi-service Rs and off-exchange backing; promote or explicitly residue-map yield-space AMM and SY-style adapters; require at least one full bond-annotated formula per major protocol class.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F2","severity":"high","section":"§3, §4 (esp. G01, G02, G03, G10, G11)","claim":"Elements pass O (implementation observability) and S (substitutability); boundaries are role-distinct.","problem":"Two competent engineers reading the same contracts will disagree on presence. Worst offenders: (1) Sh vs Ix vs Rb (cToken exchange-rate, aToken index, stETH rebase, ERC-4626 shares); (2) Pl vs Im (Aave eMode/isolation, Morpho Blue, Comet single-base); (3) In vs Rf vs Ag vs Ba (UniswapX, 1inch fusion, CoW); (4) Xm trust_domain on hybrid bridges (optimistic+validator, zk+multisig); (5) Au vs Gp/Up/allowance/session key; (6) Fl embedded in Pl/Cp flash-loan/swap vs standalone Fl; (7) Tp producer vs Ex consumer of the same pool; (8) Bs vs Sl vs absorption/reserves. Without decision procedures, formulas are non-reproducible.","fix":"Add a decision tree per contested family with positive/negative contract exemplars and a 'if both, prefer X' rule; require each core element to list canonical on-chain observables (storage, events, interface patterns) that count as presence.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F3","severity":"high","section":"§4 E041, §9, §10 ill-formed rule","claim":"Bare Xm without trust_domain is ill-formed; trust_domain is mandatory and non-defaultable.","problem":"Two incompatible value sets: E041 requires {light-client, native-consensus, external-validator, optimistic, zk-assisted, shared-sequencer-assisted}; §9 lists {code-only, counterparty, reporter/publisher, custodian, validator-set, light-client, governance, legal}. Worked formulas encode the discriminator as free isotope braces (Xm{optimistic}), not as trust_domain=. Mechanical check can only assert 'non-empty annotation' against an undefined enum; hybrid/multi-domain verifiers have no legal syntax.","fix":"Unify one closed enum; mandate Xm{trust_domain=...} (allow multi-value or primary+secondary); ship a schema validator that rejects bare Xm and unknown enum members.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F4","severity":"medium","section":"§7 naming clause, §10, L24","claim":"An informational bond is well-formed only if it names the economic consequence it enables.","problem":"Presence of [enables: ...] is a trivial syntax check; content is free text with no ontology. Any string satisfies a linter. 'Ill-formed if omitted' is enforceable; 'names the economic consequence' is a style rule nobody can enforce in CI. L24 forces —n→ for In|Rf|Ba but not for other observation edges (e.g. mempool-visible Cp/Cl).","fix":"Define a closed or extensible consequence catalog (adverse_selection, orderflow_capture, last_look, exclusivity_rent, ...); validate enum + optional free-text detail; specify which element pairs require —n→ beyond L24.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F5","severity":"medium","section":"§4 E040, §10, §12","claim":"Fl is both a P1 element and a catalyst; atomic-repayment is a state transition and leaves no terminal claim.","problem":"Conceptually defensible, but formula semantics break consistency: §10 uses Fl* only; Ag and Xm are also §12 catalysts yet appear bare in CCTP/Aave-class formulas. No production rule says when * is mandatory. Parsers cannot know whether Fl and Fl* are the same element occurrence, a role flag, or double-counting. Dual citizenship without a single syntactic form makes automated molecule validation ambiguous.","fix":"Define catalyst as a role marker on an element occurrence (always write Fl* when terminal claim is empty); list which elements may carry *; forbid bare Fl if it always catalyzes; stop listing Ag/Xm as catalysts unless formulas mark them the same way.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F6","severity":"high","section":"§17.1","claim":"Four-pass decomposition (elements → typed bonds → reaction conditions → residue) is how to use the table.","problem":"Walked on Compound v3 / Comet: Pass 1 loops on Pl vs Im and absorption as Li vs Ad vs Bs. Pass 2 has no granularity rule (every call vs element-level) and almost no —t→/—n→ guidance outside L-rules. Pass 3 is unstructured prose against §16 questions. Pass 4 has no stop condition—residue is whatever the analyst still disagrees about. Terminates only by analyst fatigue, not by a fixed point. Underspecified: identification algorithm, bond cardinality, mandatory checks against L1–L25, residue schema, when to use + vs || vs |.","fix":"Replace 17.1 with a procedure that has inputs/outputs per pass, a worked bond graph (not only an element list), exit criteria, and a machine-checkable residue record {missing_element?, contested_boundary, untyped_dependency}.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F7","severity":"high","section":"§8, §10, §11, §18","claim":"Valence predicts integration cost; molecules express elements + parameters + typed bonds + reaction conditions + residue.","problem":"Day-one implementer gaps: no BNF/JSON Schema for formulas; no valence table V(e) for the 53 elements (only prose examples); §10 'molecules' are mostly unordered element bags—only CoW shows a typed bond; no isotope key registry; no multi-chain deployment encoding; no reference suite (protocol → canonical formula) for regression; L1–L25 are not wired to a checker; no mapping element → Solidity/interface observables; operator precedence for +/||/| / bonds unspecified.","fix":"Ship (1) formal grammar + schema, (2) full valence and isotope registries, (3) ≥10 bond-complete reference molecules with tests, (4) linter for ill-formed Xm/—n→ and L-rule violations, (5) observability cheatsheet per element.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F8","severity":"medium","section":"§10 worked formulas","claim":"Worked formulas illustrate complete molecules.","problem":"Most rows omit typed bonds, reaction conditions, and residue entirely (e.g. Aave v3, Maker, GMX are '+'-joined symbols). An integrator copying this style will produce checklist tags, not an operable integration spec. Contradicts the §2 molecule definition and §7 'each bond type must be satisfied separately'.","fix":"Rewrite every §10 example with at least the required L-rule bonds, one —t→ authority edge where Up/Gp/Au exist, reaction-condition bounds, and explicit residue ∅ or listed.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F9","severity":"low","section":"§2 attributes, §4","claim":"trust_domain, layer, atom_dependency are carried on every element.","problem":"No formula syntax attaches layer or atom_dependency; atom column exists in tables but cannot appear in §10 molecules. Implementers cannot round-trip table → formula → table.","fix":"Either drop 'carried on every element' as a formula requirement or extend notation with explicit attribute fields on every element occurrence.","status":"verified"}],"strongest_thing_in_the_document":"The seven-criterion atomicity test plus the standing rejections (standards, hooks, narratives, derived observations) is a usable filter that stops the most common over-counting mistakes.","dissent_note":"Theory/audit lenses may rate X-laws and conservation highly; implementers still cannot compile or CI-check a molecule without grammar and observables."}
